## 3. Source-backed findings (external facts)

- **F1 — Bounded access exists at the reader level.** tifffile (v2026.9.20, BSD-3-Clause) reads "image data … as NumPy arrays or Zarr arrays/groups from strips, tiles, pages (IFDs), SubIFDs, higher-order series, and pyramidal levels"; its README demonstrates tile-level access (`z['0'][2, 0, 128:384, 256:]`) and dask chunked views (`dask.array.from_zarr(store, '0')`, chunk `(1,1,128,128,3)`) over an OME-TIFF. **[EF, S1]** → the 3 GB target does not require novel IO engineering; it requires the workspace to *stay on* this path (no `imread()` of whole files).
- **F2 — The >4 GB zone is where TIFF-family formats are least trustworthy.** Same README: BigTIFF "uses different file header, IFD, and tag structures with 64-bit offsets" to exceed 4 GB; ImageJ hyperstacks >4 GB keep one IFD; Zeiss LSM "wrap[s] around 32-bit StripOffsets" above 4 GB; ScanImage "optionally allows corrupted non-BigTIFF files > 2 GB"; Hamamatsu NDPI stores partial 64-bit offsets and can carry wrong tags, and its oversized JPEG tiles decode "poorly" via an MCU workaround; Micro-Manager MMStack metadata is "often corrupted or wrong". **[EF, S1]** → identity must be content-hash based, and first-contact parsing results must be surfaced to the user, not trusted silently.
- **F3 — OME-TIFF semantics live in the first IFD.** "The UTF-8 encoded OME-XML metadata found in the ImageDescription tag of the first IFD defines the position of TIFF IFDs in the high-dimensional image data" (up to 8D). **[EF, S1]** → losing that XML loses the array mapping; our importer must capture it verbatim into provenance at import time.
- **F4 — The OME data model is 5D; extra dimensions are an opt-in annotation.** The 6D/7D/8D note: extra dimensions (types `angle, phase, tile, lifetime, lambda, other`) ride inside Z/T/C via the optional `openmicroscopy.org/omero/dimension/modulo` XML annotation, and "if an application does not understand the Modulo addition, then it can treat the data as 5D, though the displayed results would need some interpretation"; plane order is governed by `DimensionOrder`. tifffile surfaces uninterpretable extents as anonymous `Q` axes (README example: `series.axes == 'QYX'` for a generic series). **[EF, S4 + S1]** → our axis model must (a) keep original per-file axis semantics verbatim, (b) show `Q`/Modulo axes explicitly, and (c) force an explicit user decision (map to a named axis / keep as opaque index / exclude) before annotations can attach — this is exactly the brief's "ambiguous axes must be shown clearly".
- **F5 — OME-ROI defines the annotation-scope convention we need.** Shapes (Point, Polygon, Polyline, Line, Rectangle, Ellipse, Label, Mask with BinData) are 2D, attached via optional `TheZ`/`TheT`/`TheC` — "if not specified then all the z-sections/timepoints/channels"; each Shape may carry a 6-value affine Transform, Fill/Stroke colors, and Text; 3D ROIs are a Union of per-plane shapes. **[EF, S5]** → adopt "index fields absent = applies to whole extent" as our scope semantics, and use OME-ROI as an export target.
- **F6 — A real issue→fix→regression-test chain exists for annotation undo (napari).** See §4. **[EF, S2, S3, S6, S7]**
- **F7 — Viewer-side display mapping is layer state, separate from data.** napari's Image-layer documentation describes contrast limits, opacity, colormaps, blending, interpolation as layer *properties*; napari's docs also maintain dedicated guides for "Scale and unit-aware rendering", "Axis names", and "Axis directions, data, and handedness". **[EF, S8 — captured page header/navigation only; deep quotes not captured]** → window/level-as-view-state is an established pattern we adopt; the deeper unit/handedness docs are a lead (L6).
- **F8 — Chunked storage with concurrent access is a solved storage primitive.** Zarr: "compressed, chunked, N-dimensional arrays", chunkable along any dimension, concurrently readable/writable from multiple threads/processes, hierarchies via groups, storable on disk/zip/S3. **[EF, S9]** → per-chunk addressing is what makes bounded IO + cache eviction tractable; our derived masks reuse the same chunking idea.

**Priority discovery that could change this plan:** F4. If any routinely acquired image in this lab carries Modulo/`Q` axes (spectral lambda, phase, tiles), the annotation scoping model must be extended beyond T/Z/C before v1, or those images must be explicitly excluded. Proposed check **[PV W7]**.

