## 4. The required issue → fix → regression-test chain (napari labels undo)

**Problem state.** napari's Labels layer (paint/fill on a label image) had no undo. PR #533 "add undo/redo to labels layer" (opened 2019-09-14 by kne42) states the gap and the UX contract: undo/redo on `Ctrl+Z`/`Ctrl+Shift+Z`; "the undo/redo stack is reset whenever you change the viewed dimensions" **[EF, S2]**

**Fix.** PR #533 was merged 2019-09-28T04:16:18Z; merge commit `d09bea9c1b36ef3d78b97b64a159bc36c1b8d9b8` onto `master` (base `dd48e8e6…`), 4 files, +116/−15, merged by sofroniewn **[EF, S2]**. The diff **[EF, S3]** shows the mechanism, which is the transferable lesson:
- `napari/layers/labels/labels.py`: `_history_limit = 100`; two deques `_undo_history`/`_redo_history`; `_save_history()` pushes `self.data[self.dims.indices].copy()` (a snapshot of the *currently displayed slice*, not the whole array — memory-bounded) and clears redo; `undo()/redo()` pop and swap snapshots; `_set_view_slice()` resets both stacks when dimensions change (consistent with the stated UX contract).
- The subtle UX part: `_block_saving` is set on mouse-press and cleared on mouse-release, so an entire paint stroke — even one that calls `paint()` hundreds of times along an interpolated path — is a **single undo step**.
- `napari/layers/labels/keybindings.py`: binds `Control-Z` / `Control-Shift-Z` to `Labels.undo`/`Labels.redo` (keyboard operability by default).
- The same PR also fixed `fill()` writing to `self.data[tuple(self.indices)]` → `self.data[tuple(self.dims.indices)]` (the new code path exposed a stale attribute).

**Regression test.** The same PR adds `test_labels_undo_redo(qtbot)` to `napari/tests/test_advanced.py`: fill → data changed; undo → equals original; redo → equals filled; set `_history_limit = 1`, one more fill, one undo succeeds, a second undo is a no-op ("cannot undo as limit exceded") **[EF, S3]**. So the chain is issue-level PR (problem statement in PR body) → merged fix commit → in-repo regression test asserting both behavior and the memory cap.

**Release applicability and limits of the chain.** Milestone "0.2"; the fix landed 2019-09-28, three days before napari's Bioimage Informatics 2019 demo per the milestone description **[EF, S2]**. I could **not** establish the exact first release containing it: `GET /releases/tags/v0.2.0` returned 404 (no GitHub Release object for that tag) **[EF, S7]**. Applicability is therefore stated at **commit level** (`d09bea9…`, base `dd48e8e…`) — anyone can verify presence with `git merge-base --is-ancestor d09bea9 <ref>` — while release-level attribution is UNCONFIRMED. I did not invent a release note.

**Second, unmerged datapoint.** PR #3155 "Proposal: override Labels.data.__setitem__ to support undo" (2021-08-13, draft, closed unmerged 2022-06-28) discusses wrapping `Labels.data` so *any* `data[i] = v` (including from plugins) lands in the undo queue, versus adding a separate `data_setitem` API **[EF, S6]**. Its unmerged status documents a durable weakness of the merged design: mutations that bypass the layer's own methods escape history. Our design consequence: undo commands are recorded by the annotation *model* API, and the model forbids out-of-band geometry mutation (single write path). **[EI]**

**Limits of transfer.** napari's snapshot-undo is per-layer, in-RAM, and unbounded in object count for shapes/points (it only covers Labels). Our annotation store is persistent, so we adopt the mechanism (slice snapshots + stroke grouping + stack reset on dimension change + hard cap with regression test) inside a command journal, not as the whole design. **[EI]**

