## 5. Precedent comparison (independence and contributed mechanisms)

| | P1: `tifffile` [S1] | P2: `napari` layers [S2, S3, S6, S8] | P3: `zarr` [S9] (supporting) |
|---|---|---|---|
| What it is | Python library (C-accelerated via imagecodecs), TIFF-family reader/writer | Qt/vispy desktop viewer; Layer abstractions (Image, Labels, Shapes, Points) | Chunked N-D array storage format + Python implementation |
| Maintainer/stack | C. Gohlke, independent; pip/conda; v2026.9.20 | napari org (community, Chan Zuckerberg Initiative lineage); Python/Qt | zarr-developers org; Python + format spec at zarr.dev |
| Depends on the other? | No viewer, no napari dependency | No format opinions; consumes array-likes | Independent format |
| Mechanism adopted | Bounded tile/page reads; explicit axes model incl. anonymous `Q`; verbatim OME-XML capture; documented limitations list; BigTIFF for >4 GB | Data/display separation (contrast/gamma as view state); stroke-grouped slice-snapshot undo with cap + regression test; keyboard bindings as first-class | Chunk-key addressing for cache/eviction and derived-mask storage |
| What it deliberately does *not* give us | No project model, no undo, no annotation identity | No file-format boundary; labels undo not persistent | No annotation semantics |
| Independence rationale | Format boundary vs. presentation layer: adopting both does not couple their versioning; each can be replaced (e.g., bioio/aicsimageio for reading, or a custom canvas) without touching the other | | |

Independence claim **[EI]**: the two primary precedents solve disjoint layers of the stack (bytes↔array vs. array↔user), come from unrelated maintainers, and are connected in the wild only through optional glue (tifffile can emit zarr; napari can consume dask/zarr). The plan borrows mechanisms, not code, from both.

