## 7. Project container, atomicity, identity, relocation

Layout **[PC]**:

```
project/
  project.json                  # authoritative manifest (schema_version, images[], styles, provenance)
  project.json.tmp-<pid>        # atomic-save staging (deleted/renamed at commit)
  annotations.jsonl             # append-only journal of committed annotation commands
  annotations.snapshot-<n>.json # periodic compaction of the journal
  images/<uuid>.meta.json       # per-image: source ref, identity, axes, calibration, import decisions, raw metadata
  cache/ exports/ logs/         # disposable / receipts / human-readable event log
```

- **Atomic save**: manifest to temp → flush+fsync → `os.replace` onto `project.json`; annotation edits are appended+fsynced to the journal per command. Recovery on open: load manifest, replay journal tail into the newest snapshot, verify counts; if the manifest is unreadable, fall back to a retained previous manifest (`project.json.bak`, written during the same save). **[EI]** — rename-atomicity mechanism executed in **[EX W2]**: an interrupted writer (truncated temp file) left the original intact and the truncated temp parseable-rejected (JSONDecodeError); a completed temp+`os.replace` swapped in the new state and removed the temp. Scope limits: user-space single-process simulation on POSIX; no power-loss, no kernel-page-cache-loss, and no directory-fsync test — the design therefore also fsyncs the directory after replace, which remains **[PV W6]** (OS-level SIGKILL/dirty-shutdown test).
- **Identity of source data**: at import, stream-compute sha256 + size (progress shown; ~10–20 s for 3 GB on HDD-class storage is a one-time cost **[EI]**); record path (absolute + project-relative), size, hash, acquisition mtime, and the verbatim metadata block (e.g., full OME-XML per **[EF S3]** locator in F3). Optional deferred hashing for huge files with `identity_status: pending` shown in the UI. **[PC]**
- **Relocation**: on open, a missing source path is resolved by trying project-relative path, then sibling directories, then a user-chosen file; a candidate is auto-accepted only if size+sha256 match, and the relink is journaled as an event. Mismatch ⇒ the image is marked `identity_changed`: annotations are preserved, the image is not displayed until the user accepts the new file explicitly. This is the direct answer to "do exported annotations still refer to the same image after transformations?" — identity is the content hash, not the path. **[PC]** (A whole-product precedent for stable entry identity — QuPath projects — was identified but its docs URL 404'd at capture time; recorded as lead L1, claim UNVERIFIED.)
- **Partial import / failed export**: imports stage into `images/<uuid>.importing/` + a draft meta record committed only on success; failure leaves a `status: failed_import` record with the raw metadata retained and the project loadable. Exports write to `exports/<id>/` and are atomically renamed into place with a receipt; a failed export leaves the receipt with `status: failed` and no partial output at the final path. **[EI]**
- **Bundle vs. external reference (the trade-off)**: `import_mode: copy` (small files; bundle is self-contained) vs. `reference` (default for ≥512 MB sources; avoids duplicating 3 GB per bundle). A bundle with references is not self-contained; `verify_bundle` tool lists referenced files with hashes so a receiver knows what must travel together. **[PC]** — brief explicitly asks for this trade-off; we choose explicit mode per image, never silent copying.
- In-place modification of sources is forbidden v1; adapters open read-only. **[PC]**

